#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205011} :activity/id #uuid "f13da08f-0333-5cf7-8f52-a79e6bf0ebd6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048550 :topic/id #uuid "a9165535-d837-58b0-9784-39a400db9d86" :topic/math-academy-id 5869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205011 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205186 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we defined the gradient of a scalar-valued function with respect to a vector. In this lesson, we extend this idea to functions where the input is a *matrix* and the output is a scalar.\n\nSuppose we have a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a matrix $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$ to a scalar value $f(A)$. Just like with vector inputs, we define the **gradient of $f$ with respect to $A$** as the matrix of partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to each individual entry of $A{:}$\n\n![](images/0b/0b3ce184c982fe85eb25f79161431b7620227b96909d87998fa82efbb469423c.svg)\n\nThis gradient has the same shape as the matrix $A.$ It tells us how sensitive the output $f(A)$ is to changes in each element $a_{ij}$.\n\nLet’s apply this to a concrete example. Suppose we define the function\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))\n$$\n\nfor a square matrix $X \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 2}$. Let's find the gradient of $f$ with respect to $X$ and evaluate it at a specific point.\n\nFirst, we write the matrix $X$ as\n![](images/67/67c8cc92409c6bd1595fda93c8711ce91a40d220526cc52ee9a8f4d3842f7354.svg)\nThen, the determinant of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\det(X) = x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}.\n$$\n\nSo, the function becomes\n\n$$\nf(X) = \\ln(x_{11}x_{22} - x_{12}x_{21}).\n$$\n\nTo find the gradient, we differentiate $f$ with respect to each element of $X.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{11}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{12}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{21}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{22}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{11}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{12}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{21}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{22}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}_{22}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & -\\frac{{x}_{21}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} \\\\ -\\frac{{x}_{12}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}} & \\frac{{x}_{11}}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}_{11}{x}_{22}-{x}_{12}{x}_{21}}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{det(X)}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{22} & -{x}_{21} \\\\ -{x}_{12} & {x}_{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we wish to evaluate this gradient at\n![](images/93/93200d9d52e6a58a52e4b9f65db6bc13ded0c6e17dbb8fc0381ae40081932ff9.svg)\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(X) & =3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6-5 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our values into our gradient. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{X}f(X) & =-\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{11}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result tells us how the scalar output $f(X) = \\ln(\\det(X))$ would change with respect to small changes in each entry of the matrix $X$. Each element in the gradient matrix captures that local rate of change." :tutorial/id #uuid "4ace4da9-e2b2-56f1-b064-78e25344a30e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12175 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "51d89ff0-2e51-5813-be29-4f290760f1f7" :step/math-academy-id 450917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204079 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203857 :question/id #uuid "ca1002c8-c615-5f7b-8876-82cf87c8864f" :question/math-academy-id "e-17349"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c676a83b-f3d4-579b-ab54-de2104552e8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050484}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534003}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function With Respect to a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "5682316e-09ab-5709-825d-abbce84fe179" :step/math-academy-id 450918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205192 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a scalar-valued function defined by\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{m \\times n}$.\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of $f$ with respect to the matrix $A$. The result turns out to be\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nLet’s verify this by working component-wise. \n\nFirst, note that we can write the product $\\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x$ as a double summation as follows:\n\n$$\nf = \\mathbf y^T A \\mathbf x = \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l.\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw how to do this for $\\mathbf x^T A \\mathbf x$. The idea here is similar.\n\nNow, we differentiate this summation with respect to $A_{ij}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial A_{ij}} \\left( \\sum_{k=1}^m \\sum_{l=1}^n y_k A_{kl} x_l \\right)\n$$\n\nSince only the term where $k = i$ and $l = j$ depends on $A_{ij}$, all other terms vanish. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial A_{ij}} \n= y_i x_j.\n$$\n\nThis tells us that the entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is $y_i x_j$. Hence, the full gradient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_A f = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nThis result is an example of an outer product. The gradient of $\\mathbf{y}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the matrix $A$ is given by multiplying the column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ by the row vector $\\mathbf{x}^T$, resulting in a matrix of the same shape as $A$.\n\nLet’s look at a few examples to see this in action." :tutorial/id #uuid "957b6db8-152d-50d1-9685-1b280888ce3d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12388 :tutorial/title "Component-Wise Differentiation"} :step/id #uuid "40001fda-db52-5314-a824-eba6f4cc9e69" :step/math-academy-id 450921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205014}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204080 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203871 :question/id #uuid "5aa67862-6f7a-57ac-9473-d039fc4bfec4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17951"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35dc17b6-1c82-55ec-9657-20f2850794e5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542190}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Gradient of a Function Component-Wise"} :step/id #uuid "ad3cf801-8dd4-5f9a-b7ca-588e439c66cc" :step/math-academy-id 450922 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205195 :tutorial/content "When working with scalar-valued functions defined on matrices, it's often helpful to compute gradients using component-wise differentiation and the chain rule. This approach breaks down the computation into simpler intermediate steps and allows us to clearly see the dependencies.\n\nConsider a *scalar* function $L(\\mathbf A),$ defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\mathbf{A}) = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)^T \\left( \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2 \\times 3}$, $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nSuppose we want to compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L$, which is a matrix of the same shape as $\\mathbf{A}$. The entry in the $i$th row and $j$th column of the gradient matrix is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}.\n$$\n\nTo simplify the differentiation, we define $\\mathbf y \\in\\mathbb R^{2\\times 1}$ as \n![](images/d1/d14a8e7752fdc2f20c9c325c5a0076d79a86ccd2a0c46d6b353ce53c3f1561d7.svg)\n\nThen, we can write $L$ in terms of $\\mathbf y$ as \n\n$$\nL = \\frac{1}{2} \\mathbf{y}^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\dfrac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}}$, we apply the multivariable chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a_{ij}} = \\sum_{k=1}^2 \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y_k}{\\partial a_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNote that the summation is from $k=1$ to $k=2$ because $\\mathbf y$ has two components, and we're differentiating with respect to each of them.\n\nNow, let's analyze the two factors of the product that appear in this summation.\n\n- We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y}L & ={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial y_k} = y_k.\n$$\n\n- Writing $\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{b}$ in component form\n\n$$\ny_i = \\sum_{j=1}^3 a_{ij} x_j - b_i,\n$$\n\nwe have\n![](images/76/761731b884eee7282e01bda6964861fc8f3d4a3a44bfec09fda478170982b56a.svg)\nOnly the $k$th row of $\\mathbf{A}$ affects $y_k$.\n\nPutting our two results together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause all terms vanish except the one where $k=i$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{A}} L = \\mathbf{y} \\cdot \\mathbf{x}^T.\n$$\n\nIn the next example, we will use this formula to compute the gradient for specific vectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a8a781f-98de-5bf4-8f02-80c10f3211b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12479 :tutorial/title "Differentiation Using the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "da5331f6-b68e-5fed-80d1-95b4e0971765" :step/math-academy-id 450925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204081 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203866 :question/id #uuid "429a6a34-2fe5-5c09-9b74-e4b650209007" :question/math-academy-id "e-17870"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7b2139-06eb-5584-a267-d9fbc9b5d041" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542179}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Calculate Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "0dbe27ec-63c8-5d16-a0de-f9deb9e9f3e8" :step/math-academy-id 450926}] :activity/title "Matrix Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204182}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542274} :activity/id #uuid "2404189e-9656-51cd-a3b5-6c13f1ff213f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048550 :topic/id #uuid "a9165535-d837-58b0-9784-39a400db9d86" :topic/math-academy-id 5869} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "312a355a-904b-5eca-ae0f-c9e77ad2f94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -9 \\\\ 36 & 36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541523} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa26cf7-587f-542e-bf6a-224a38d8a577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541555} {:answer/id #uuid "c54ab21b-6f6b-5453-8f98-703092e388ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -18 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541838} {:answer/id #uuid "d64795ea-1505-54f8-ad01-cafe6fb7fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541880} {:answer/id #uuid "d830e853-f6f7-552e-b9bf-8e3dfedadf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -9 & 36 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541523} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd1be76d-c3b1-5ea5-bbf6-626285d50488" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6779795b-e35d-5389-887e-4355a853f59a" :question/math-academy-id "q-269388" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}3{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}=3{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=3(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=3([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 3\\end{aligned}])=3([\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -3 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -9 \\\\ 36 & 36\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "117f03df-7ecf-55d7-878d-f554adc908c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542257}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed298814-70d0-5656-b6e2-258ce3641ddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-992" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541924} :answer-field/id #uuid "00abc28b-95f9-5105-a5d2-782d2db3d65a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541963} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7d3c641-98ec-536a-b7ca-05afeccd2ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541915} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fefe5d8-100f-5282-b59e-0d1be2e84a1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43f1cf78-e476-5744-a4e1-4eb640602151" :question/math-academy-id "q-269517" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(4Ax-5b)^{T}(4Ax-5b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$832$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=4Ax-5b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=4Ax-5b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=4{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-5{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=4{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 4{x}_{j} \\\\  & =8{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $8$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=8y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =8y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4Ax-5b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8([\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}208 \\\\ -248\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 832 \\\\ 0 & -992\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15fbb940-fc98-53cc-ba92-f859f422626f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "976542d7-dd7f-5daf-aab6-bb5f2d5588dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541737} {:answer/id #uuid "9c769625-2afb-52e2-9006-b7df92b8cd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541748} {:answer/id #uuid "a54b3b29-5e7b-5355-993d-c1692b182224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541763} {:answer/id #uuid "af1b5240-994c-562c-9dae-36bc16150a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541781} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9d05e7-08ac-5c52-8f0b-08fb9cac8514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541748} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6e05b4e-adf0-5ec5-b639-9d019a4a9d63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae4e117c-08e3-5ee2-be97-840ec47e6061" :question/math-academy-id "q-269381" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4fd46510-de50-56a6-8167-99996004b4ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542233}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542257 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e1e29d1-1888-52aa-8dc6-4b2845f73611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541611}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541611} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa668de-a737-5f91-800a-6c4b031f88c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33107365-8d72-59e7-84e4-fb7248465cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541529} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd131918-eda5-51aa-9124-e861cbcfd218" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a276eb8e-dc20-5069-8231-5e2d2d2a281a" :question/math-academy-id "q-269429" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66630ed2-72bb-5fad-a04e-d80e51de9e27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7257e47e-0bbc-5f6f-8e0d-fa05a95547d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541656} {:answer/id #uuid "736cbadd-3b7b-553e-ac3e-54c9d0aa1309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 70 & 140 \\\\ 0 & 80 & 160\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541658} {:answer/id #uuid "90109471-65f3-534e-ab7f-25d38145cf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 70 & 80 \\\\ 140 & 160\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541713} {:answer/id #uuid "9df978fd-8d29-5189-99c3-1b1404d874c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 70 \\\\ 20 & 80\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541751} {:answer/id #uuid "d4aa7742-e290-58bb-8b89-9a097ad03132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541658} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8abf9ca-ece0-5a55-b508-ff5cc5e98d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "be0cbc5f-85e4-5a3d-bf50-d49616a38b87" :question/math-academy-id "q-268341" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)={y}^{T}Ax$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 10 & 20\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 70 & 140 \\\\ 0 & 80 & 160\\end{aligned}].\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b6011297-6460-5